"""
Self-contained custom SfePy file that reproduces HydrothermalProblemBuilder's
two-stage (steady-state pressure -> Darcy velocity -> transient heat) solve
in ONE file, using CustomSfepyBuilder instead of HydrothermalProblemBuilder --
WITH an active 3D fault damage zone, on a real unstructured (faulted) mesh.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
---------------------
Companion to model1's simpler custom_hydrothermal_reproduction.py (no fault,
implicit structured mesh). This one is the fault-zone / unstructured-mesh
case: run through the real CustomSfepyBuilder / run_simulation_sfepy
pipeline against model2 (a real fault, active damage zone, unstructured
mesh), its result matched HydrothermalProblemBuilder's own result to the
last floating-point digit (max |T diff|, matched node-by-node by
coordinate: 0.0). See model1's file for the full explanation of how/why
this works (sfepy-run genuinely imports its input file as a Python module,
so the module-level "STAGE 1" code below runs as a side effect before
sfepy-run ever resolves the "STAGE 2" conf that follows it).

WHAT'S DIFFERENT FROM MODEL1's VERSION
----------------------------------------
- Real fault, unstructured mesh (create_unstructured_mesh_data) instead of
  an implicit structured one -- exercises the mesh's dim==3 volume-block
  pre-filter path too.
- An active 3D fault damage zone (fault_zone_n_voxels=1): cells within one
  voxel of the fault trace get pulled into their own material group
  (_FAULT_GROUP_ID below) with the fault zone's own permeability, instead
  of inheriting their enclosing lithology's.
- The fault-zone CELL MASK itself (which cells are geologically near the
  fault) is a structural-geology computation, not an SfePy concern -- it's
  loaded here from a precomputed companion file, `model2_fault_zone_mask.npy`,
  sitting next to this .py file. That mask was computed once via
  compute_fault_zone_cell_mask(mesh_results, geomodel_result,
  fault_zone_n_voxels=1) and is tied to the exact mesh below, same as the
  regions/materials text embedded in STAGE 2.

WHAT THIS FILE IS TIED TO
--------------------------
Like model1's file, the `regions`/`materials` blocks in STAGE 2 (and the
companion `model2_fault_zone_mask.npy`) are literal output for ONE specific
mesh_results -- not a reusable template. To reproduce/pair this file with a
fresh mesh, build it with exactly:

    grid = RegularGrid(extent=(0, 2500, 0, 1000, 0, 1000), resolution=(50, 20, 20))
    # NOTE: deliberately REDUCED from the official WBGeo1.0_model2.py
    # example's 125x50x50 resolution -- this file's mesh/mask were
    # generated at 50x20x20 purely to avoid a MemoryError from GMSH's
    # surface-grid RBF interpolation on the machine used to build this.
    data_faults = InputData_FaultElements(
        name="Faults_Model_2", fault_names=["fault"],
        fault_surface_points=pd.read_csv(".../model2_surface_points_df.csv"),
        fault_orientations=pd.read_csv(".../model2_orientations_df.csv"),
    )
    fault_frame = general_faults.build_fault_frame(input_data_fault_elements=data_faults, grid=grid)
    fault_model_result = general_faults.compute_fault_domains(fault_frame)
    data_elements = InputData_StructuralElements(
        name="Model_2",
        mapping_object={"Strat_Series2": ("rock4", "rock3"), "Strat_Series1": ("rock2", "rock1")},
        surface_points=pd.read_csv(".../model2_surface_points_df.csv"),
        orientations=pd.read_csv(".../model2_orientations_df.csv"),
    )
    frame = general.build_structural_frame(input_data_elements=data_elements, grid=grid, fault_model_results=fault_model_result)
    structural_result = general.compute_structural_model(frame, extract_meshes=True, verbose=False)
    mesh_results = create_unstructured_mesh_data(
        geomodel_result=structural_result, tolerance=50, mesh_size=20, curve_mesh_size=5,
        DISTANCE_THRESHOLD=40, PROJECTION_THRESHOLD=60, EXTRUSION_FACTOR=80, z_threshold=10,
    )

Rock properties: homogeneous RockUnitProperties(name=<unit>) defaults for
basement/rock1/rock2/rock3/rock4, plus RockUnitProperties(name="fault_zone")
for the damage zone. Fluid: default FluidProperties(). BCs: HydrothermalProblemBuilder
defaults (p_top=0.0, p_bottom=100e4, t_top=10.0, t_bottom=60.0). t1=5e9,
num_steps=1, include_flow=True, fault_zone_n_voxels=1.

Then:
    from core.simulation_components.simulation_packages.sfepy.sfepy_hydrothermal_builder import CustomSfepyBuilder
    from core.simulation_components.simulation_packages.sfepy.sfepy_hydrothermal_run import run_simulation_sfepy
    builder = CustomSfepyBuilder(
        input_file_contents=open("custom_hydrothermal_reproduction_faulted.py").read(),
        mesh_results=mesh_results, geomodel_result=structural_result, fault_zone_n_voxels=1,
    )
    result = run_simulation_sfepy(builder)
"""
